<section class="reference-section" id="mysterious-yaksha"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>The Mysterious Yaksha and the Gods</h2><a href="/mysterious-yaksha.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Kena Upanishad · Gita Press, Gorakhpur.</strong> Sanskrit text with Shankara-bhashya and Hindi explanation, from the owner-supplied 190-page scan. This is an original condensed English reader, not a translation. The private scan is not distributed by this website.</p><ol><li><strong>3.1–2 · supplied PDF pp. 142–146</strong><small>Brahman’s victory, the devas’ claim to greatness and the appearance of the unknown Yaksha.</small></li><li><strong>3.3–6 · PDF pp. 147–151</strong><small>Agni names himself and his power, but cannot burn the blade of grass.</small></li><li><strong>3.7–10 · PDF pp. 152–154</strong><small>Vayu names himself and his power, but cannot move the blade of grass.</small></li><li><strong>3.11–12 · PDF pp. 155–157</strong><small>Indra approaches, the Yaksha vanishes and Uma Haimavati appears.</small></li><li><strong>4.1 · PDF pp. 158–159</strong><small>Uma identifies the victory as Brahman’s and Indra understands.</small></li><li><strong>4.2–3 · PDF pp. 160–162</strong><small>Agni, Vayu and Indra are distinguished by their nearness to and recognition of Brahman.</small></li><li><strong>4.4–6 · PDF pp. 163–170</strong><small>Lightning, the blink of an eye, mental recollection and the name Tadvanam.</small></li><li><strong>4.7–8 · PDF pp. 171–180</strong><small>The teacher confirms the instruction and names disciplined practice, the Vedas and truth as its foundation, limbs and abode.</small></li></ol><p>The reader preserves the narrative order of the encounters while condensing the commentary. “Brahman” is distinguished from Brahma. Uma Haimavati is presented as the teacher who reveals the identity of the Yaksha. All three images are newly AI-generated interpretations, each used once; figures, clothing, light and landscape are imagined. The hero is explicitly composite because the text sends Agni, Vayu and Indra in sequence. The reflection question is editorial.</p></div></section>
